WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.377
Assessment begins by identifying data that must remain confidential for a long time.

00:00:05.377 --> 00:00:10.248
Teams should look for regulated records, health information, legal material, government data,

00:00:10.248 --> 00:00:13.495
sensitive personal information, authentication secrets, strategic business information,

00:00:13.495 --> 00:00:16.337
research and development data, and long-term archives.

00:00:16.337 --> 00:00:20.396
Then they should map the systems that transmit, store, replicate,

00:00:20.396 --> 00:00:22.425
and back up that data.

00:00:22.425 --> 00:00:25.267
Without that mapping, harvest-now, decrypt-later stays abstract.

00:00:25.267 --> 00:00:28.108
The next step is reviewing cryptographic dependencies.

00:00:28.108 --> 00:00:30.544
Which TLS endpoints protect the data?

00:00:30.544 --> 00:00:35.415
Which VPNs, SSH services, secure email systems, storage products, cloud services, APIs,

00:00:35.415 --> 00:00:37.444
and partner integrations are involved?

00:00:37.444 --> 00:00:41.097
Which libraries, certificates, key management systems, hardware security modules,

00:00:41.097 --> 00:00:44.345
and vendor products do those systems rely on?

00:00:44.345 --> 00:00:47.998
The answer often spans multiple teams and ownership boundaries,

00:00:47.998 --> 00:00:53.275
which is why this work should not live only in a security spreadsheet.

00:00:53.275 --> 00:00:56.116
Prioritization should consider sensitivity, confidentiality lifetime, volume,

00:00:56.116 --> 00:00:59.769
external exposure, mission importance, dependency complexity, and migration difficulty.

00:00:59.769 --> 00:01:04.640
A public API carrying sensitive records for long-term retention may deserve earlier

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:08.294
attention than an internal test endpoint with synthetic data.

00:01:08.294 --> 00:01:14.788
A difficult-to-migrate legacy system may also deserve early planning because lead time is part of risk.

00:01:14.788 --> 00:01:19.659
The highest priority is often where value, exposure, and slow migration overlap.

00:01:19.659 --> 00:01:23.718
Risk assessment should involve security, engineering, legal, privacy, compliance, operations,

00:01:23.718 --> 00:01:24.936
and business stakeholders.

00:01:24.936 --> 00:01:28.183
Legal and privacy teams can explain data obligations.

00:01:28.183 --> 00:01:30.619
Engineering teams can identify real dependencies.

00:01:30.619 --> 00:01:34.272
Operations teams can describe change windows and reliability constraints.

00:01:34.272 --> 00:01:36.302
Business owners can clarify impact.

00:01:36.302 --> 00:01:36.952
Together,

00:01:36.952 --> 00:01:41.823
they can separate theoretical risk from actionable risk and build a migration

00:01:41.823 --> 00:01:44.420
roadmap that is realistic enough to execute.
